Give chrome://settings the new tab page's design.

Two things Settings gets that the other WebUI pages do not: a card drawn as a
hairline ring rather than a shadow, and a section label in the design's own
treatment. The palette underneath both now lives in
aerium-webui-style.patch, in cr_shared_vars.css, so every WebUI page shares it;
what is left here is only what is genuinely about this page.

THE CARD. settings_page/settings_section.css puts --cr-card-shadow on #card, so
a box-shadow with no blur and one pixel of spread draws exactly the ring the
design asks for, through a token that is already in place: transparent fill,
ring, 18px radius. A border would have meant editing an element whose padding
upstream has tuned.

It is here rather than in the shared file because Settings is the page where a
card is one whole group, and that is not true elsewhere. history_item.css draws
its list as a single segmented card - #background split across is-card-start and
is-card-end, with a box-shadow drawn deliberately past the edge so the rows join
- and a ring token there would put a line between every row.

THE SECTION LABEL is the only piece of type on the page that names a group
rather than a setting, and the design says that with 11px, half a pixel of
tracking, upper case and the accent - the same treatment the new tab page's
settings panel gives its own headings. It keeps var(--cr-primary-text-color) as
the fallback so the file still reads correctly on its own.

html:root in both places for the same reason as in the shared file: the
html[webui-refresh-2026] block in cr_shared_vars.css sets
--cr-card-background-color, and a bare html would lose to it on specificity.

Verified against the real 152.0.7977.82 sources: both files fetched at that tag,
edited, the patch generated from the pair, then both patches applied in series
order to a clean copy of all three files - they land with no fuzz and reproduce
every one byte for byte. chrome/browser/resources/settings runs stylelint over
its own CSS during the build, and pre-flight 39 failed on it once already
(color-hex-length wanted #FFF); the rules that could bite were checked by hand
again here - no collapsible hex, no duplicate custom property in a block, a
trailing newline, and every new rule preceded by a comment.

--- a/chrome/browser/resources/settings/settings_page/settings_section.css
+++ b/chrome/browser/resources/settings/settings_page/settings_section.css
@@ -19,16 +19,21 @@
   padding-inline-end: var(--cr-section-padding);
 }
 
+/* Aerium: the section label is the one on the new tab page's own settings
+ * panel - small, spaced, upper case, in the accent - rather than a slightly
+ * larger run of body text. It is the only piece of type on this page that
+ * names a group rather than a setting, and this is how the design says that. */
 #header .title {
-  color: var(--cr-primary-text-color);
-  font-size: 108%;
-  font-weight: 400;
-  letter-spacing: .25px;
+  color: var(--aerium-accent, var(--cr-primary-text-color));
+  font-size: 11px;
+  font-weight: 600;
+  letter-spacing: .5px;
   margin-bottom: 12px;
   margin-top: var(--cr-section-vertical-margin);
   outline: none;
   padding-bottom: 4px;
   padding-top: 8px;
+  text-transform: uppercase;
 }
 
 #feedback {
--- a/chrome/browser/resources/settings/settings_vars.css
+++ b/chrome/browser/resources/settings/settings_vars.css
@@ -30,3 +30,27 @@
     --settings-error-color: var(--google-red-300);
   }
 }
+
+/* --- Aerium: the card shape, on the one page whose card markup has been read.
+ *
+ * The palette itself lives in cr_shared_vars.css and reaches every WebUI page;
+ * this is only the structural half of the design, and it is here rather than
+ * there because Settings is the page where a card is one whole group.
+ * settings_page/settings_section.css puts --cr-card-shadow on #card, so a
+ * box-shadow with no blur and one pixel of spread draws exactly the hairline
+ * ring the design asks for, through a token that is already in place. A border
+ * would have meant editing an element whose padding upstream has tuned.
+ *
+ * History draws its list as one segmented card and would grow a ring between
+ * every row, which is why this is not in the shared file.
+ *
+ * html:root for the same reason as there - it has to outweigh the
+ * html[webui-refresh-2026] block that sets --cr-card-background-color. */
+html:root {
+  --cr-card-background-color: transparent;
+  --color-webui-card-background: transparent;
+  --cr-card-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--aerium-outline);
+  --cr-card-border-radius: 18px;
+  --cr-dialog-border-radius: 16px;
+  --iron-icon-fill-color: var(--aerium-label);
+}
